  "query_preview": "..At 230 PM, there are scattered thunderstorms mostly over\nYavapai County. An area of low pressure over northern Mexico is\ndrifting northward into southern Arizona. With well above average\nmoisture remaining over our area, we expect another round of \nscattered showers and thunderstorms tonight, with the highest \ncoverage (40-60%) along and south of the Mogollon Rim/White Mtns \nand 10-30% to the north. This is generally east of the I-17/US89 \ncorridor. With slow storm motion and plenty of available moisture,\nthe main threat with today's activity will be locally heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding. By Friday, the upper low moves \nnortheast of our area with a drier westerly flow behind it. This \nwill bring a decrease in storm activity, with isolated to \nscattered showers/storms over the eastern half of the state. \nSaturday is looking storm free at this time as even drier air \nmoves in from the west. \n\nSunday through Monday - a trough developing along the west coast\nis forecast to move eastward across our region. Moisture\nassociated with two tropical cyclones (Hurricane Polo forecast to\nbe off the southern Baja at this time and TS Odalys much farther \nwest) will be pulled northeastward into the southwest United \nStates in advance of this trough. There is still plenty of model \nspread regarding the depth/speed of the developing trough as well \nas the location of the deepest moisture plume from the south. \nAlthough shower and thunderstorm chances will increase quickly by \nlate Sunday through Monday, it's still too early to tell how heavy\nthe rainfall might be and where the heaviest rain will be placed.\nConfidence has edged enough higher to place a Slight Excessive\nRainfall Outlook for Monday/Monday night. The timing of the \ntrough combining with the moisture would need to line up just \nright for this to happen, but it's not out of the question. Around\n20-30% of the ensembles from the latest 00Z runs show a heavy \nrain signal (1-2\"+) for our area, though almost all show at least\nsome rainfall. Rain chances linger into Tuesday and will depend\non the timing of the trough, then dry weather looks to return by \nmid week.",
